# Keep both backup scripts in sync

`rsync_tmbackup.sh` and `rsync_tmbackup_exfat.sh` are the two supported entry points. A change to shared behavior in one is incomplete until the other script has the matching change.

## Required behavior

- In the same turn, port the shared change to the other script and to the matching test file (`tests/test_standard.sh` or `tests/test_exfat.sh`) when that behavior is covered there.
- Do this unless the user explicitly said, before the edit, not to update the other script. Silence, or discovering a difference while editing, is not an opt-out.
- When the user opts out, leave the other script untouched and say which change was left unsynced.

## Keep these differences

Do not copy filesystem-specific behavior across the scripts unless the user asked for that design change.

Standard (`rsync_tmbackup.sh`):

- Hard links and `--link-dest`. Default flags include `-D`, `--hard-links`, `--links`, `--perms`, `--owner`, and `--group`.
- `latest` is a symlink, written only after rsync exits 0.
- Interrupted recovery uses `backup.inprogress` and the newest timestamped directory. `--resume-last` is refused while that file exists.
- Automatic expiration is on, including deleting the oldest snapshot when the disk is full.
- With no older snapshot to hard-link, say the interrupted snapshot is being continued. Do not say a new backup is being created.

ExFAT (`rsync_tmbackup_exfat.sh`):

- Full independent copies. No hard links or `--link-dest`. Default flags use `--copy-links` and `--modify-window=2`, and omit `-D`, `--hard-links`, `--perms`, `--owner`, and `--group`.
- `latest.txt` names the active snapshot and is written before rsync starts. There is no `latest` symlink.
- Interrupted recovery requires both `backup.inprogress` and `latest.txt`. `--resume-last` updates the snapshot named by `latest.txt`.
- Never automatically delete snapshots, including when the disk is full.

```bash
# Shared: both scripts must honor a non-zero rsync status
RSYNC_EXIT_CODE=$?
```
